%[text] ![](text:image:3b80)
%[text] ## Why?
%[text] - Used to parameterize functions we pass to MATLAB solvers (ode45, fminsearch, etc.).   This allows a wider variety of design implementations.  We can programmatically vary the constants/coefficients in the models.
%[text] - Can be used as a more succinct way to specify a handle to a simple function, as an alternative to defining a local function or creating a separate file with a standard function.   You will see this in the MATLAB documentation examples and or what gen AI provides. \
%[text] ## Example: Multidisciplinary Optimization of Ship Shafting
%[text] **Problem**: Design (choose "best" parameters) to minimize wear, vibration and whirling of propulsion shaft design.
%[text] **Model:**
%[text] ![](text:image:728b)
%[text] 
%[text] ![](text:image:8331)
%[text] 
%[text] **Parameter Search:**
%[text] ![](text:image:2f9a)
%[text] 
%[text] 
%[text] **Challenge:** 
%[text] - How can we solve the model (a set of diff. eqs) for a variety of potential designs (model parameters)? \
%[text] **Anonymous functions allow us to pass model parameters into the problem formulation to simulate many possible designs and optimize the results.**
%[text] 
%[text] ## Syntax: [Anonymous Function](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/matlab_prog/anonymous-functions.html)
%[text] The syntax is dense:
%[text] ```
%[text] HANDLE = @(ARG1, ARG2, ..., ARGN) EXPRESSION;
%[text] ```
%[text] Some things to keep in mind about creating anonymous functions:
%[text] - The result is to create a new variable, HANDLE, of data type [function\_handle](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/function_handle.html).
%[text] - This new function has input arguments specified as (ARG1, ARG2, ..., ARGN)
%[text] - The EXPRESSION is a single executable statement
%[text] - The default behavior is that the anonymous funciton will return one output. \
%[text] For example
hypotenuse_handle = @(a, b) sqrt(a^2 + b^2)
%%
%[text] We can use the function handle as we've seen before
h = hypotenuse_handle(3,4)
%%
%[text] ### Using anonymous function as a rate function with ODE solvers
%[text] Instead of using a separate function as the rate function, we could use an anonymous function.  For example,
%[text] Consider a simple model of a speed control of a vessel
%[text] ![](text:image:227f)
%[text] $m\\frac{dv(t)}{dt} + b \\, v^2(t) = F\_\\text{engine}$
%[text] with constants
%[text] - Vessel mass: $m=1000 \\, \\text{kg} $
%[text] - Drag coefficient: $b = 50 \\, \\text{N}/(\\text{m/s}^2)$
%[text] - Engine thrust $F\_{\\text{engine}} = 2000 \\, \\text{N}$ \
%[text] We can write the rate function model as
%[text] $\\dot{v} = 1/m(-b \\, v^2 + F\_{\\text{engine}})$
%[text] which we can implement as
rfh = @(t,v) 1/1000 * (-50*v^2+2000)
%[text] Then we can pass this new function handle to the ODE solver
[tt, vv] = ode45(rfh, [0 20], 0);

plot(tt,vv)
grid("on")
xlabel("Time [s]")
ylabel("Speed [m/s]")
%[text] ### 
%%
%[text] ### Using variables in the EXPRESSION.
%[text] Unlike a separate expression (which has its own local scope), an anonymous function has access to the variables in the workspace.  For example, consider a quadratic function where the coefficients, a, b, c, are specified outside the function.
%[text] $y = a x^2 + bx +c$
a = 1;
b = 1;
c = 1;
quadratic_handle = @(x) a*x.^2 + b*x +c;
q = quadratic_handle(1)
%%
%[text] What will happen if we re-define a constant and then call the anonymous function?
a = 2;
q2 = quadratic_handle(1)
%%
%[text] What if we also redefine the anonymous function?
a = 2;
quadratic_handle = @(x) a*x.^2 + b*x +c;
q3 = quadratic_handle(1)
%%
%[text] Which allows us to programmatically modify the constants 
figure;
xx = linspace(0,10,100);
a = 1;
c = 2;
for b = 1:5
    % Redefine our function with the new constants.
    quadratic_handle = @(x) a*x.^2 + b*x +c;
    % Call the function
    plot(xx, quadratic_handle(xx))
    hold on
end
hold off
xlabel("X [n/a]")
ylabel("Quadratic handle [n/a]")
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![](text:image:9a18)
%[text] ```
%[text] flowchart TD
%[text]     F[Define xx as linspace from 0 to 10 with 100 points. 
%[text]     Define constants for a and c.]
%[text]     F --> G{{"Loop over b from 1 to 5"}}
%[text]     
%[text]     %% Creating a subgraph for the loop
%[text]     subgraph for loop
%[text]         G -->|new param b| H[New function handle with current values of a, b and c]
%[text]         H --> I[Plot the function for the current b value]
%[text]     
%[text]         I --> J[Hold on to overlay plots]
%[text]         J --> G
%[text]     end
%[text] ```
%%
%[text] ## Use-Case: Passing a parameterized anonymous function to a solver
%[text] We can put these two ideas together to parameterize the rate function so that we can vary the parameters of the model.
%[text] Consider an engineering design problem where we want to evaluate a few different designs, each with a different drag coefficient, and predict how varying the drag coefficient will affect the response.
%[text] **Problem:** Simulate the response of our vessel model for a range of values in the range $b = \[40-60\]\\, \\text{m/s}$.
%[text] We can solve this by using a loop to do the interation and creating a new function handle for each iteration.
clf;
bb = linspace(40, 60, 5);
for ii = 1:length(bb)
    % Assign parameters
    b = bb(ii);
    m = 1000;
    F = 2000;
    % Create new function handle with new parameters
    rfh = @(t,v) 1/m * (-b*v^2+F);
    % Simulate, AKA solve the ODE
    [tt, vv] = ode45(rfh, [0 20], 0);
    % Visualize
    plot(tt,vv,"DisplayName",sprintf("b = %.1f",b))
    hold on
end
legend("Location","southeast")
grid("on")
xlabel("Time [s]")
ylabel("Speed [m/s]")
%[text] 

